                    "comment": "t.cat - join tensors along an axis they have"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 863,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 1016,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Joins tensors end to end along an axis they already have.\n\nUse it to put two pictures side by side or one above the other, to append new rows to a table, or to join two lists into one longer list. The result has the same number of axes as the inputs - it is just longer along the one you join on. (To keep the tensors apart as layers of a new axis, use t.stack.)\n\nTHE SHAPES MUST FIT:\nThe tensors must match in every axis except the one being joined: two 7 by 6 tensors join along dim 0 into 14 by 6, or along dim 1 into 7 by 12. A 7 by 6 and a 3 by 6 join along dim 0 (10 by 6), but not along dim 1.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.cat <number of inputs: int>\nDefault 2. The argument is how many tensor inlets the node has, NOT the\ndim: t.cat 3 joins three tensors. (t.cat 0 or t.cat 1 gives a node with\nonly one inlet, which just passes its tensor through.) The dim is set\nin the dim box.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.cat 2\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor 1:\nThe first tensor - it goes first (on top, or on the left). Receiving it makes the node join and send. A list or numpy array becomes a float32 tensor.\n\ntensor 2, tensor 3, ...:\nThe others, in order. They are only stored, so they must arrive BEFORE tensor 1. Each is converted to tensor 1's dtype and device.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nThe axis to join along: 0 (the default) joins below - more rows; 1 joins to the right - more columns. Negative counts from the end. A dim the tensors do not have sends nothing. Changing it does NOT send by itself;\nin the demo a metro inside make_F re-sends the F ten times a second.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe joined tensor.\n\nIF A SHAPE DOES NOT FIT:\nUnlike np.concatenate, t.cat does not stop: a tensor whose shape does not fit is LEFT OUT and the rest are joined. Nothing is said unless the app's verbose setting is on; then the console names the input that was left out (tensor 2, tensor 3 ...). With two inputs that means tensor 1 comes out alone, unchanged - so if the output is the same size as tensor 1, check the other's shape. The same happens before anything has reached tensor 2.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\ntensor 1 is the F (7 by 6), tensor 2 a ramp of the same shape. With dim 0 the ramp hangs below the F (14 by 6); type 1 into dim and it sits to its right (7 by 12). t.info shows the shape.\n\nRELATED:\nt.stack joins along a NEW axis. t.vstack and t.hstack are t.cat with the axis chosen for you. t.chunk and t.tensor_split cut a tensor into pieces - the reverse. np.concatenate is the numpy version.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 1000,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Joins tensors end to end along an axis they already have.\n\nUse it to put two pictures side by side or one above the other, to append new rows to a table, or to join two lists into one longer list. The result has the same number of axes as the inputs - it is just longer along the one you join on. (To keep the tensors apart as layers of a new axis, use t.stack.)\n\nTHE SHAPES MUST FIT:\nThe tensors must match in every axis except the one being joined: two 7 by 6 tensors join along dim 0 into 14 by 6, or along dim 1 into 7 by 12. A 7 by 6 and a 3 by 6 join along dim 0 (10 by 6), but not along dim 1.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.cat <number of inputs: int>\nDefault 2. The argument is how many tensor inlets the node has, NOT the\ndim: t.cat 3 joins three tensors. (t.cat 0 or t.cat 1 gives a node with\nonly one inlet, which just passes its tensor through.) The dim is set\nin the dim box.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.cat 2\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor 1:\nThe first tensor - it goes first (on top, or on the left). Receiving it makes the node join and send. A list or numpy array becomes a float32 tensor.\n\ntensor 2, tensor 3, ...:\nThe others, in order. They are only stored, so they must arrive BEFORE tensor 1. Each is converted to tensor 1's dtype and device.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nThe axis to join along: 0 (the default) joins below - more rows; 1 joins to the right - more columns. Negative counts from the end. A dim the tensors do not have sends nothing. Changing it does NOT send by itself;\nin the demo a metro inside make_F re-sends the F ten times a second.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe joined tensor.\n\nIF A SHAPE DOES NOT FIT:\nUnlike np.concatenate, t.cat does not stop: a tensor whose shape does not fit is LEFT OUT and the rest are joined. Nothing is said unless the app's verbose setting is on; then the console names the input that was left out (tensor 2, tensor 3 ...). With two inputs that means tensor 1 comes out alone, unchanged - so if the output is the same size as tensor 1, check the other's shape. The same happens before anything has reached tensor 2.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\ntensor 1 is the F (7 by 6), tensor 2 a ramp of the same shape. With dim 0 the ramp hangs below the F (14 by 6); type 1 into dim and it sits to its right (7 by 12). t.info shows the shape.\n\nRELATED:\nt.stack joins along a NEW axis. t.vstack and t.hstack are t.cat with the axis chosen for you. t.chunk and t.tensor_split cut a tensor into pieces - the reverse. np.concatenate is the numpy version."
                },
